      "chapterTitle": "I. The 09:15 Arrival on Brabourne Road",
      "heading": "I. The 09:15 Arrival on Brabourne Road",
      "body": "The revolving door of the State Export House on Brabourne Road was framed in quarter-inch cast brass that had been polished every morning at seven o'clock since the building opened in November 1962.\n\nTo enter it, you stepped into one of the four curved glass quadrants, placed the flat of your right palm against the smooth brass push-bar, and allowed your forward momentum to overcome the counterweights in the ceiling. It required exactly four pounds of sustained pressure to start the rotation. On humid monsoon mornings, when the river mist rolled off the Hooghly and coated the glass with a greasy film of diesel exhaust and river water, the rubber draft-strips along the perimeter squeaked against the granite threshold with a sharp, pneumatic hiss: *shhh-tuck, shhh-tuck*.\n\nBhabesh Mukherjee had stepped into that revolving door at nine-fifteen on thirty-one years of working mornings.\n\nHe performed the motion for the last time on Monday, September 30, 2024.\n\nHe was wearing his retirement clothes: a freshly laundered cream-colored tussar silk kurta with small mother-of-pearl buttons that his wife Anindita had bought for him in Gariahat three weeks earlier, a pair of well-pressed dark charcoal trousers, and his twenty-year-old black leather Bata slip-ons that had been resoled three times by the cobbler outside the General Post Office. In his left hand, he carried a light green nylon shopping bag containing an empty plastic tiffin box, a small umbrella with a wooden curved handle, and a brass key ring holding two keys that would cease to belong to him at five o'clock in the afternoon.\n\nHaripada, the lift attendant who had operated the brass-caged Otis elevator since 1989, stood beside the manual sliding gate with his white cotton gloves resting on the brass lever.\n\nHaripada did not ask for his floor. He had not asked for his floor since the winter of the Cargill grain shipments in 1996.\n\n\"Good morning, Mukherjee-babu,\" Haripada said, pulling the lattice gate shut with a metallic rattle. \"Fourth floor today?\"\n\n\"Fourth floor, Haripada,\" Bhabesh said, nodding gently. \"As usual.\"\n\nThe cage rose with its familiar, hydraulic groan, the greased guide-rails smelling of graphite, sulfur, and eighty years of damp river silt. Through the open iron lattice, the floors clicked past in rhythmic succession: ground floor, tea registry; second floor, jute export inspectorate; third floor, marine insurance and customs clearances; and then the fourth floor, where the letters painted on the frosted transom window read in faded gilt: *DIRECTORATE OF ACCOUNTS & SPECIAL REVENUE LEDGERS*.\n\nBhabesh stepped out onto the grey linoleum runner.\n\nThe corridor was fifty yards long, lit by tubular fluorescent lights suspended from high, vaulted ceilings that had once been cooled by punkah fans before independence. On both sides of the corridor, the wooden doors stood half-open, revealing cavernous offices filled with green Godrej steel cupboards, wooden desks with inkwells recessed into the oak corners, and rows of clerks in short-sleeved terrycloth shirts sitting behind towers of brown paper file folders tied with red cotton tape.\n\nEvery single person who looked up from their desk knew that today was Bhabesh's final day.\n\nThey did not shout congratulations; government export houses do not conduct retirement with brass bands. They looked at him with that specific, subdued, reverent melancholy with which churchgoers look at a stained-glass window being removed for restoration. They nodded; they raised their right hands in silent salute; two junior typists stood up from their manual Godrej typewriters and touched their palms together in a quiet *namaskar*.\n\nBhabesh walked past them with his eyes fixed on the door at the end of the hall.\n\nCabin 402.\n\nThe brass nameplate on the frosted glass door was four inches long by twelve inches wide: *Bhabesh Mukherjee, M.Com., Chief Accounts Documentation Officer*. It had been screwed into the teak stile on March 4, 1993, replacing the nameplate of one Sudhir Ranjan Roy, who had died of coronary arrest at his desk during the great shipping strike of 1992.\n\nBhabesh unlocked the brass rim lock, stepped inside, and closed the door behind him.",
