      "id": "sec-eo-1b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "I-B. The Apprenticeship Under the Gas Lamps",
      "heading": "I-B. The Apprenticeship Under the Gas Lamps",
      "body": "When Bhabesh had entered the department as a twenty-nine-year-old probationary clerk in March 1993, the fourth floor still retained the physical atmosphere of the post-war imperial administration.\n\nThere were no desktop computers. There were no plastic ballpoint pens.\n\nThe Chief Documentation Officer, Sudhir Ranjan Roy, had sat behind the Burma teak desk with two bottles of ink: blue-black fountain-pen ink imported from Pelikan in Germany for general correspondence, and crimson carmine ink manufactured by Sulekha in Jadavpur for regulatory discrepancies and customs holds. Every probationary clerk was assigned a wooden high stool with a wicker seat, placed directly in front of the master ledgers, and required to spend six months copying export manifests before being permitted to initial an official receipt.\n\n\"A manifest, Mukherjee,\" Sudhir Roy would say, dipping his steel nib into the inkwell with a sharp, dry click, \"is not a piece of paper. It is a legal confession. When a merchant in Calcutta declares that twenty crates contain Darjeeling pekoe and not low-grade dust from the Terai, he is pledging his credit against the honor of the port. If you sign without verifying the tare weight, you are an accomplice to perjury.\"\n\nSudhir Roy had possessed a legendary memory for shipping vessels.\n\nHe knew the draft, the deadweight tonnage, and the boiler capacity of every merchant ship that entered the Hooghly between Diamond Harbour and the Kidderpore lock gates. He could tell by looking at a bill of lading whether a freighter named the *SS Clan Macinnes* or the *Jalavijaya* had sailed with an overloaded deck cargo of raw timber from the Andaman Islands, simply by calculating the fuel consumption between Haldia and Sandheads.\n\nBhabesh had spent three years sitting on that wicker stool, learning how to listen to the sounds of the building.\n\nHe learned that when the wooden beams in the ceiling groaned with a low, twisting sound at three in the afternoon, it meant the tide was rising at Princep Ghat, pushing the river barges against the wooden pilings of the jetties. He learned that when the peons brought out the brass coal braziers in December, the smoke would drift along the vaulted ceiling like blue fog, making the eyes water until you learned to blot your ledgers with your left sleeve while holding the pen with your right.\n\nThose three years had formed Bhabesh's moral spine.\n\nHe had learned that an institution is not held together by ministers or managing directors who arrive in white Ambassador cars with yellow flashing lights. An institution is held together by obscure men who sit on high stools, who never appear in newspapers, who check the arithmetic of third-generation tea merchants and refuse to move a decimal point for anyone.",
