      "id": "sec-eo-3",
      "type": "text-image-right",
      "heading": "II. The Anatomy of Cabin 402",
      "body": "Cabin 402 was fourteen feet wide by eighteen feet deep, with a single high sash window looking north toward the brick turrets of the General Post Office and the distant spires of St. Andrew's Kirk.\n\nIn thirty-one years, Bhabesh had altered almost nothing about the room's physical geography.\n\nAlong the west wall stood four olive-green Godrej almirahs, each secured by a brass padlock manufactured by the Godrej Security Works in Vikhroli, Bombay. Inside those cupboards, arranged in chronological order from April 1993 to August 2024, were eleven thousand four hundred and ninety-two bound ledgers of export documentation. Every single consignment of Darjeeling tea, Assam CTC fannings, raw jute bales from Cooch Behar, tanned goat skins from Tangra, and shellac flakes from Purulia that had left the docks of Kidderpore or Netaji Subhash Dock had passed through these four cupboards.\n\nOn the south wall hung a framed mechanical print of the Port of Calcutta in 1898, presented to him by the dock workers' welfare committee in 2004, and a bakelite wall calendar from the State Bank of India whose red plastic date-slider had remained positioned on September 15 for three weeks.\n\nIn the center of the room sat his desk.\n\nIt was an imperial-sized executive desk of seasoned Burma teak, five feet wide by three feet deep, with nine drawers arranged in two pedestals and a center pencil drawer that stuck in rainy weather. The green linoleum desk-blotter was permanently stained with dark circular rings where thirty thousand porcelain cups of milky canteen tea had been set down during the monsoon hearings.\n\nOn the left side of the blotter sat the brass paperweight.\n\nIt was shaped like a nineteenth-century maritime anchor, cast in solid admiralty brass, weighing two pounds and four ounces. It had been presented to him on March 4, 2008, by the General Manager of the Export Council to commemorate his fifteen years of continuous service. On its flukes, engraved in fine serif lettering, was the inscription: *B. Mukherjee — Steadfast in Record, 1993–2008.*\n\nFor sixteen years, that anchor had pinned down every single document of importance that crossed his desk.\n\nWhen the cyclone of 1999 blew out the sash window and scattered five hundred pages of tea invoices across the corridor, the anchor had remained motionless on the center blotter, holding down the master ledger of the Assam Tea Corporation like a rock in a mountain river.\n\nBeside the anchor was his pen holder—a turned block of rosewood holding two blue Reynolds ballpoint pens, a flat ivory paper knife, and a wooden-handled rubber stamp that read in bold, purple capitals: *EXAMINED & RECORDED — CHIEF DOCUMENTATION OFFICER*.\n\nBhabesh sat down in his high-backed revolving chair.\n\nThe seat was upholstered in dark green rexine that had cracked along the front edge, exposing the coarse yellow horsehair padding beneath. The spring mechanism beneath the seat gave a short, metallic groan—a high-pitched *krrrk* that Bhabesh had heard every morning of his adult life.\n\nHe placed his palms flat against the green blotter.\n\nThe teak wood was cold beneath his skin. Outside the window, a tram bell clanged twice on Brabourne Road, its hollow brass chime reverberating off the red-brick façade of the Reserve Bank annex across the street.\n\n\"Thirty-one years,\" Bhabesh whispered to the empty room.\n\nHe did not speak with pride. He spoke with the quiet, bewildered astonishment of a passenger who has fallen asleep on a train in the evening and woken up at dawn in a desert terminal where the tracks end in a buffer of sand.",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1450133064473-71024230f91b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=85",
      "alt": "Heavy mahogany executive desk with empty drawers pulled slightly ajar under warm overhead lighting",
      "caption": "The brass paperweight shaped like a ship's anchor, presented at the fifteen-year service milestone in 2008.",
      "imageSize": "portrait",
      "imageWidth": 1200,
      "imageHeight": 1800
