      "id": "sec-eo-3b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-B. The Great Tea Crisis of October 1998",
      "heading": "II-B. The Great Tea Crisis of October 1998",
      "body": "To understand why Cabin 402 mattered, one had to remember the third week of October 1998.\n\nA depression in the northern Bay of Bengal had collided with an unseasonal cyclone from Orissa, dumping four hundred millimeters of rain across lower Bengal in forty-eight hours. The Hooghly had crested the embankment at Garden Reach; the railway freight yards at Shalimar were submerged beneath two feet of muddy water; and forty-two thousand chests of first-flush Darjeeling tea were trapped in the transit godowns of the Port Trust.\n\nIn London and Hamburg, buyers from Twinings and Lipton were threatening to cancel five million pounds sterling in forward contracts if the certificates of origin and moisture-clearance manifests were not telexed by midnight on Friday.\n\nThe General Manager had suffered an ulcer flare-up and was admitted to Belle Vue Clinic.\n\nThe Deputy Director was stranded in his flooded bungalow in Salt Lake.\n\nBhabesh Mukherjee, then thirty-four years old with two years in Cabin 402, had rolled his trousers to his knees, taken off his shoes, tied his leather satchel around his neck with his necktie, and walked through four miles of knee-deep water from his rented flat in Bhowanipore to Brabourne Road.\n\nThe building had no electricity. The emergency diesel generator in the basement had drowned under six feet of water.\n\nBhabesh had climbed forty-four flights of dark stone stairs using a kerosene hurricane lamp borrowed from Haripada. In Cabin 402, he had lit four white candles, placed them in empty teacups, and spent thirty-six continuous hours reconciling the railway consignment notes with the port inspectorate vouchers.\n\nHe had checked every single invoice by hand: the garden name, the invoice number, the net weight in kilograms, the moisture percentage tested at the Siliguri laboratory, the customs bond serial number.\n\nHis fingers had cramped so severely around his fountain pen that every three hours he had to plunge his right hand into a bucket of cold rainwater to unfreeze the tendons.\n\nAt four o'clock on Friday morning, the senior director of Brooke Bond India, a tall Englishman named Mr. Henderson who had stayed in Calcutta after the tea plantations were nationalized, had arrived in Cabin 402, his linen suit soaked to the waist, smelling of Scotch whisky and panic.\n\n\"Mukherjee,\" Henderson had rasped, leaning his palms against the green blotter. \"The German buyers are canceling at six. If those eighty manifests don't clear, forty estates in the hills will not be able to pay the Durga Puja bonus to sixty thousand tea pluckers next Monday. There will be riots in Kurseong.\"\n\nBhabesh had not looked up from his ledger.\n\nHe had signed sheet seventy-nine. He had applied the brass anchor to hold the paper flat. He had picked up the wooden rubber stamp, inked it on the purple pad, and struck the bottom of the page with a clean, sharp blow: *EXAMINED & RECORDED*.\n\n\"The eighty manifests are signed, Mr. Henderson,\" Bhabesh had said, his voice quiet, calm, as if he were discussing the weather on an ordinary Tuesday. \"I have verified the moisture certificates. The tea has not spoiled. The telex operator in the GPO has power from an emergency army battery. Tell London the cargo is sound.\"\n\nHenderson had stared at the young officer in the candlelit room.\n\nHe had reached into his coat pocket, taken out an expensive gold Swiss chronometer, and placed it on the desk: \"Take this, Mukherjee. You saved the industry tonight.\"\n\nBhabesh had picked up the gold watch with two fingers, placed it back into Henderson's wet coat pocket, and patted the lapel.\n\n\"I am an officer of the Government of West Bengal, Mr. Henderson,\" Bhabesh had said. \"We do not take watches for doing our duty. Give the sixty thousand pluckers their Puja bonus.\"\n\nThat was the night Cabin 402 became an institution.\n\nFor twenty-six years after that night, whenever a crisis occurred—a bank strike in Bombay, a dock collapse at Haldia, a new tariff regime imposed by the World Trade Organization—the files were brought to Bhabesh. Nobody asked why he took forty minutes to examine an invoice that other officers cleared in four. They knew that if Bhabesh Mukherjee's purple stamp was on the manifest, the cargo was as solid as the basalt stone of the Victoria Memorial.",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497366216548-37526070297c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=85",
      "alt": "A stack of vintage ledger books with hand-bound spines and brass clasps",
      "caption": "The tea consignment records of October 1998, preserved in Cupboard One under four layers of brown paper.",
      "imageSize": "landscape",
