      "id": "sec-eo-3c",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-C. The Ledger Binders of Baithakkhana Road",
      "heading": "II-C. The Ledger Binders of Baithakkhana Road",
      "body": "The physical ledgers themselves were masterpieces of an endangered urban craft.\n\nEvery March, before the start of the new financial year, Bhabesh would walk down to Baithakkhana Road near Sealdah station to commission the sixteen master registers required by Cabin 402.\n\nBaithakkhana Road was a narrow lane that smelled of boiling bone glue, hide trimming, turpentine, and freshly pressed cotton rag paper. In small, open-fronted workshops lit by single naked lightbulbs, four generations of Muslim bookbinders from Murshidabad worked on wooden benches polished by two centuries of craft.\n\nBhabesh's binder was an elderly man named Ustad Nooruddin, who wore steel-rimmed spectacles tied behind his ears with black cord.\n\nNooruddin did not use machine-made paper.\n\nHe sourced eighty-pound handmade ledger paper from a mill in Puducherry that used unbleached cotton comber rags. It was paper that had a creamy, slightly ribbed surface—paper that could absorb iron gall ink without bleeding, paper that could survive eighty monsoons without yellowing, paper that would outlive the grandchildren of the men who wrote on it.\n\n\"Look at this sewing, Mukherjee-babu,\" Nooruddin would say, running his withered thumb along the spine of a four-hundred-page volume.\n\nHe used five-strand linen cord imported from Belfast, waxed with natural beeswax to resist silverfish and paper mites. The spine was reinforced with split calfskin leather from the tanneries of Tangra, died a deep, lustrous burgundy. The corners of the heavy strawboard covers were capped in brass triangular plates to protect against the constant friction of being pulled from metal shelves.\n\nOn the front cover, Nooruddin would tool the title in gold leaf, heating the individual brass type over a charcoal brazier until they hissed when touched with a wet rag:\n\n*STATE EXPORT CLEARANCES — VOLUME IX — JUTE & FIBRE FRACTION.*\n\n\"A computer file,\" Nooruddin had remarked to Bhabesh in the spring of 2012, when the first pilot project for digital documentation had been announced with great fanfare in the newspapers, \"a computer file is like a breath on a cold window. It looks bright for three seconds, and then someone turns off the electricity and it is gone. But this book... if you throw this book into the Hooghly, it will take twenty years for the river to dissolve the ink.\"\n\nBhabesh had smiled then, thinking the old binder was simply defending his obsolescence.\n\nNow, standing in Cabin 402 with Sourav's tablet resting on the blotter, Bhabesh looked at the four green cupboards.\n\nNooruddin was dead. His workshop on Baithakkhana Road had been replaced by a mobile-phone accessories shop selling tempered-glass screen protectors and Chinese plastic charging cables. The linen cord from Belfast was no longer imported. The eighty-pound rag paper from Puducherry was no longer milled.\n\nThe craft was extinct.\n\nAnd next Monday, sixteen thousand pages of hand-bound calfskin ledgers would be loaded into a five-ton Tata truck, driven across the Vivekananda Setu to a tin-roofed warehouse in Dankuni, and stacked on industrial steel racks where no human hand would ever open them again.",
