      "id": "sec-eo-6",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "III. The Successor with the Tablet",
      "heading": "III. The Successor with the Tablet",
      "body": "At ten o'clock, the frosted glass door opened.\n\nSourav Datta stepped into Cabin 402.\n\nSourav was twenty-eight years old. He had joined the department eleven months earlier from an IT services consultancy in Salt Lake Sector V, carrying a Master's degree in Cloud Information Architecture from an institute in Pune. He was wearing slim-fit beige chinos, a navy polo shirt with the company logo embroidered on the left chest, clean white running shoes, and wireframe titanium spectacles that looked as if they weighed less than a postcard.\n\nUnder his left arm, Sourav carried an Apple iPad in a magnetic charcoal case. He did not carry a pen.\n\n\"Good morning, Bhabesh-da,\" Sourav said, pulling the visitor's cane chair forward with a single finger. \"Happy last day. How are you feeling?\"\n\nBhabesh smiled his courteous, institutional smile.\n\n\"Good morning, Sourav. I am feeling normal. Sit down.\"\n\nSourav sat down, placed the tablet on the blotter, tapped the screen with his index finger, and unlocked it with facial recognition. A clean, white grid of dashboard widgets flickered into existence: *Port Authority API Status: Operational; Clearance Latency: 420ms; Pending OCR Audits: 0*.\n\n\"Bhabesh-da,\" Sourav said briskly, his voice full of the unburdened velocity of the digital generation. \"Everything for the handover is mapped in the cloud migration tracker. The National Single Window System integration is ninety-eight percent complete. By next Monday, when the new fiscal quarter starts, no physical paper will enter Cabin 402. The shipping lines will upload their bills of lading directly via the EDI gateway, the customs clearance will be verified by SHA-256 digital signature, and the port clearance certificate will be issued automatically to the vessel captain's mobile device within three minutes.\"\n\nHe announced this as if he were delivering a miraculous prophecy.\n\nBhabesh looked at the young man's smooth, uncreased face. He looked at the four green Godrej almirahs along the west wall.\n\n\"And if the server in Delhi goes down, Sourav?\" Bhabesh asked softly.\n\n\"We have three availability zones, Bhabesh-da,\" Sourav smiled with patient amusement, the way an astronomer might explain the lunar orbit to a child who fears the dark. \"Mumbai, Hyderabad, and Frankfurt. If Delhi fails, the load switches to Hyderabad in eighty milliseconds. There is zero single point of failure.\"\n\n\"I see,\" Bhabesh said.\n\nHe opened the center drawer of his desk. He took out the two master keys—the heavy brass keys with the red electrical tape wrapped around the stems.\n\n\"These are for the cupboards,\" Bhabesh said, placing them on the blotter beside Sourav's iPad. \"Cupboard One has the tea registers from 1993 to 2000. Cupboard Two has the jute export ledgers. Cupboard Three has the leather and marine claims. Cupboard Four has the special exemption certificates signed by the Commerce Ministry during the foreign exchange crisis of 1991.\"\n\nSourav looked at the brass keys as if they were stone flint arrowheads recovered from an archaeological ditch.\n\n\"Right,\" Sourav said, nodding politely. \"The facilities team will move the cupboards to the archives warehouse in Dankuni next weekend. We are converting Cabin 402 into a digital collaboration hub with a video-conferencing screen and four hot-desks for the field inspectors.\"\n\nBhabesh did not flinch.\n\nHe had known about the hot-desks since July. The Assistant Director of Administration had shown him the architectural drawings over tea in the canteen. The teak desk was to be auctioned off as surplus colonial-pattern furniture; the frosted glass door was to be replaced by a frameless tempered glass panel with acoustic seals; and the brass nameplate was to be handed to Bhabesh as a personal souvenir.\n\n\"The lock on Cupboard Two sticks when the humidity exceeds eighty percent,\" Bhabesh said quietly, looking at the keys. \"You must push the door inward with your knee while turning the key to the left. If you force it, the internal tumbler will jam, and the locksmith from Bowbazar charges seven hundred rupees to open it.\"\n\nSourav tapped the iPad screen. A note appeared: *Dankuni transfer — physical archives — keys with Facilities.*\n\n\"Got it, Bhabesh-da,\" Sourav said, not looking up. \"Thanks.\"",
