      "id": "sec-eo-8",
      "type": "image-left-text",
      "heading": "IV. The Brass Ship's Anchor",
      "body": "By two in the afternoon, the handover formalities were completed.\n\nSourav had generated the digital sign-off certificate on his tablet; Bhabesh had signed his name on the glass screen using a rubber-tipped stylus that felt greasy and unresponsive against the oleophobic surface. His signature, which had possessed a sharp, aristocratic flourish with a fountain pen in 1993, looked like the electrocardiogram trace of a dying animal—spastic, disconnected, humiliatingly crude.\n\nSourav left to attend an all-hands product demonstration on the sixth floor.\n\nBhabesh remained alone in Cabin 402.\n\nHe stood up from his chair, walked to the sash window, and looked down at Brabourne Road.\n\nThe street was boiling with the late-afternoon commercial frenzy of central Kolkata. Handcarts loaded with twelve-foot rolls of brown corrugated cardboard were being hauled across the intersection by two men with bare, sweating torsos; yellow Ambassador taxis were honking with rhythmic, desperate impatience; pedestrians carrying plastic folders were weaving between moving trams and municipal garbage trucks.\n\nNone of them looked up.\n\nFor thirty-one years, Bhabesh had derived his quiet, profound sense of dignity from the knowledge that this immense, roaring machinery of commerce depended on him.\n\nIf Bhabesh Mukherjee refused to sign a manifest on a Friday afternoon because the weight of tea chests from Darjeeling did not reconcile with the rail transit slip from Siliguri, two thousand tons of cargo remained anchored at Garden Reach. The shipping agents from Mackinnon Mackenzie would send their senior managers down to Cabin 402 with tins of imported British biscuits and polite smiles; the customs superintendents would ring his extension with respectful inquiries; the port commissioners would wait for his memorandum before issuing clearance papers.\n\nHe had not been corrupt. In thirty-one years, he had never accepted a single rupee of speed-money, a bottle of Scotch whisky, or a basket of Alphonso mangoes from a freight forwarder.\n\nHis power had resided in his precision.\n\nHe was the guardian of the record. He was the man who knew that page 412 of the 1998 register contained the precedent that prevented the seizure of seventy-two crates of orthodox leaf tea during the Asian financial crisis. He had been indispensable.\n\nNow, he watched a handcart haul a load of PVC pipes into an alley beside the Tea Board building.\n\nThe handcart did not pause. The port did not wait. The cloud in Frankfurt had taken over his precision, and it required neither tea nor pension.\n\nHe walked back to his desk, took out a cardboard carton he had retrieved from the stationery room, and began to pack.",
