      "chapterTitle": "V. The Farewell Tea in Room B",
      "heading": "V. The Farewell Tea in Room B",
      "body": "At three-thirty, the departmental peon, Gopal, knocked on the door: \"Mukherjee-babu, Director-sir is calling everyone to Conference Room B.\"\n\nConference Room B was on the third floor.\n\nIt was a windowless chamber with fake wood panelling, forty molded plastic chairs, and an overhead projector that had been turned off since the pandemic. Around the oval table, sixty employees of the State Export House were standing with paper napkins in their hands, eating cold vegetable patties from a bakery on Canning Street and small, greasy gulab jamuns served in plastic cups.\n\nThe Managing Director, an IAS officer named Sanjoy Chakraborty who had been posted to the department eleven months earlier and would likely be transferred to the Animal Husbandry Ministry before the next monsoon, stood at the head of the table.\n\nHe was reading from a printed sheet of paper handed to him by his private secretary:\n\n\"Today we bid farewell to Shri Bhabesh Mukherjee... Chief Accounts Documentation Officer... thirty-one years of meritorious service... a pillar of integrity... witnessed the transition from manual ledger operations to the National Single Window System... we wish him a long, healthy, and spiritually fulfilling retired life.\"\n\nThe Director smiled. The sixty clerks clapped politely, their applause muffled by the acoustic ceiling tiles.\n\nThe Director handed Bhabesh a brass commemorative plate engraved with the state seal, a shawl of red synthetic fleece wrapped in cellophane, and a bouquet of six yellow gladioli whose stems were leaking brown water onto the paper doily beneath.\n\n\"Mukherjee-babu,\" the Director smiled, checking his gold wristwatch, \"would you like to say a few words?\"\n\nBhabesh stepped forward.\n\nHe looked at the sixty faces in the room. He saw men who had joined as clerks when they had black hair, who now had silver hair and bifocals. He saw young women who had never used carbon paper in their lives, who were staring at their mobile screens while balancing their gulab jamun cups on their knees.\n\nHe cleared his throat.\n\n\"Thank you, Director-sir,\" Bhabesh said. His voice was quiet, completely level, without a tremor. \"An office does not remember who swept the floor or who signed the manifest. The building only remembers the weight of the paper. I have spent thirty-one years making sure that when the government said something was true, it was true down to the last rupee and the last kilogram. I hope the cloud remembers that as well as the cupboards did. Thank you.\"\n\nHe bowed slightly.\n\nThere was a moment of awkward, startled silence. The young women stopped looking at their phones. The old clerks looked at their paper napkins.\n\nThen the Director clapped once, briskly: \"Very well said, Mukherjee-babu! Very poetic. Tea, everyone, please enjoy the tea.\"",
