      "chapterTitle": "VI. Clearing the Bottom Drawer",
      "heading": "VI. Clearing the Bottom Drawer",
      "body": "By four-fifteen, the conference room was empty, and Bhabesh was back in Cabin 402.\n\nHe opened the bottom-right drawer of his desk.\n\nThis was the drawer that had never contained an official document. It was his private museum—the personal sediment deposited by eleven thousand working days.\n\nHe lifted the items out one by one, placing them into the cardboard box:\n\nFirst, his backup spectacles from 2006: round wire frames with bifocal lenses that made the floor look slightly curved. He had kept them in a velvet-lined tin case that once contained digestive lozenges from a chemist in Bowbazar.\n\nSecond, a heavy green woollen muffler that his mother had knitted for him in the winter of 1994, during his second year at the department, when the draft from the sash window used to give him stiff necks. His mother had been dead for sixteen years, but the wool still smelled faintly of the camphor tablets she used to wrap inside her saris.\n\nThird, an identity card issued on March 4, 1993, by the Government of West Bengal. The black-and-white passport photograph showed a young man of twenty-nine with dark, thick hair parted severely on the left, wearing a striped necktie and a collar that was slightly too large for his neck. The plastic laminate had cracked in 2011, and Bhabesh had mended the corner with two inches of yellowed cellotape that had crystallized into brittle amber over thirteen monsoons.\n\nFourth, the brass anchor paperweight. He wrapped it in three sheets of old newspaper and placed it in the center of the box, wedging it between the muffler and the tin case so it would not roll.\n\nFifth, a small porcelain teacup with a chipped blue rim that had belonged to his friend Dilip Sen, who used to sit in Cabin 401 until he retired in 2018 and died of pancreatic cancer six months later in a flat in Barrackpore.\n\nThe drawer was empty.\n\nBhabesh pushed it shut. The brass drawer-pull clicked into place with that dry, solid *clack* he had heard forty thousand times.\n\nHe stood in the center of the room with the cardboard box in his arms.\n\nThe room was quiet. The four Godrej almirahs stood silent against the wall, their green paint reflecting the dull, grey light of the late afternoon sky. The desk was bare: no ledgers, no files, no red tape, no brass paperweight. On the green blotter, the circular ring marks from thirty thousand cups of tea looked like the fossilized footprints of an extinct creature that had lived on this timber for three decades and vanished without leaving a bone.\n\nThe door opened.\n\nGopal the peon stood in the doorway holding a screwdriver: \"Mukherjee-babu... Facilities said to remove the nameplate before five.\"\n\nBhabesh looked at the peon.\n\n\"Yes, Gopal,\" Bhabesh said, his voice soft, gentle, entirely devoid of rancor. \"Take it off.\"\n\nGopal unscrewed the two brass screws from the teak stile. The metal made a dry, screeching sound as the threads backed out of the wood. The nameplate came away in the peon's hand, leaving behind two clean, pale rectangles on the dark teak where thirty-one years of sunlight had never fallen.\n\nGopal handed the brass plate to Bhabesh.\n\n\"Keep it safe, Mukherjee-babu,\" Gopal said, his eyes moist. \"You were a good officer. You never shouted at the peons.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Gopal,\" Bhabesh said. He placed the brass plate into the box, on top of the green muffler. \"Take care of your knees.\"",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1450133064473-71024230f91b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=85",
      "alt": "An empty office drawer pulled open with dust and shadows inside",
      "caption": "The bottom drawer of Cabin 402, empty for the first time since March 1993.",
      "imageSize": "landscape",
