      "id": "sec-eo-13",
      "type": "text-image-right",
      "heading": "VII. Ramu's Tea Corner at 17:15",
      "body": "At five-ten, Bhabesh walked out of Cabin 402, walked down the long corridor, and stepped into Haripada's elevator for the final descent.\n\nThe cage dropped with its heavy hydraulic sigh: third floor, second floor, ground floor.\n\nHaripada pulled the lattice gate open. He stood at attention, his white cotton gloves at his sides: \"Pranam, Mukherjee-babu.\"\n\n\"Pranam, Haripada,\" Bhabesh said. He reached into his pocket, took out a five-hundred-rupee banknote, and pressed it into the old attendant's palm. \"Buy sweets for your grandson.\"\n\nHe walked through the lobby. He stepped into the cast-brass quadrant of the revolving door. He placed his right palm against the smooth bar. He pushed.\n\n*Shhh-tuck.*\n\nThe door rotated. He stepped out onto the granite steps of Brabourne Road.\n\nThe evening air was hot, thick, smelling of roasted groundnuts, tram lubricant, and the sulfurous exhaust of hundred-year-old buses. The noise of the city hit him like a physical wave—not with hostility, but with that massive, roaring indifference that is Kolkata's true signature.\n\nHe did not walk immediately toward the BBD Bagh tram terminus.\n\nHe walked twenty yards down the footpath, beneath the shade of an ancient neem tree whose roots had buckled the pavement slabs, to Ramu's tea stall.\n\nRamu had boiled tea on that corner since 1987. He had a blackened aluminium kettle that hissed over a kerosene Primus stove, a wooden bench polished by thirty thousand trouser seats, and a glass case containing cream crackers and salted biscuits.\n\nBhabesh set the cardboard box down on the wooden bench. He sat down beside it.\n\n\"A cup of special tea, Ramu,\" Bhabesh said. \"Less sugar.\"\n\nRamu poured the tea from high above his head, the steaming stream of dark amber liquid falling into an earthen clay cup (*bhar*) without spilling a single drop. He handed the cup to Bhabesh with a small piece of newspaper to hold the hot clay.\n\n\"Mukherjee-babu,\" Ramu said, wiping his hands on his apron. \"Retirement today?\"\n\n\"Retirement today, Ramu,\" Bhabesh said.\n\nHe sipped the tea. The tea was hot, sweet, pungent with bruised ginger and cardamom.\n\nHe sat on the bench for forty minutes. He watched the employees of the State Export House come out through the revolving doors in twos and threes, talking about train timings, dinner menus, and cricket scores. They walked past him with their umbrellas tucked under their arms. Some saw him and waved; others were looking at their phones and did not see the man sitting on the wooden bench with a cardboard box on his knees.\n\nAt six o'clock, the streetlights flickered on along Brabourne Road—a long, golden chain of sodium-vapor lamps reflecting against the wet tram lines.\n\nBhabesh finished his tea. He placed the empty clay cup into the wicker bin beneath the bench.\n\nHe picked up his cardboard box.\n\nThe box was heavy. Two pounds of brass anchor; an old identity card; a green woollen muffler; thirty-one years of paper.\n\nHe walked toward the tram terminus.\n\nTomorrow morning, at nine-fifteen, the revolving brass door on Brabourne Road would turn four times. Haripada would pull the lattice gate shut. Sourav Datta would tap his tablet screen in Mumbai and Frankfurt. And Bhabesh Mukherjee would be sitting in his flat in Garia, drinking tea from a porcelain cup, learning how to look at the sky at noon without an invoice to sign.",
