      "id": "sec-eo-13b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "VII-B. The Tram Across the Maidan",
      "heading": "VII-B. The Tram Across the Maidan",
      "body": "The tram from BBD Bagh to Gariahat was Route 25.\n\nBhabesh climbed into the first-class compartment of car number 582. The tram was a wooden-bodied carriage built in 1968 at the Nonapukur workshops, painted the municipal colors of sea-green and pale cream, its interior fitted with slatted teak benches, brass window catches, and overhead rattan pull-straps suspended from enameled iron rods.\n\nHe placed the cardboard box on the wooden slats beside his thigh.\n\nThe conductor, a man named Nirmal with a leather coin pouch slung across his chest, rang the brass bell twice: *ting-ting*.\n\nThe electric motor hummed beneath the wooden floorboards, a low, magnetic vibration that rose through Bhabesh's shoe soles and entered his bones like an old song. The steel wheels squeaked as the tram rounded the loop outside the General Post Office, crossed the intersection of Council House Street, and entered the vast, dark expanse of the Maidan.\n\nIt was six-twenty in the evening.\n\nThe Maidan was breathing after the heat of the day. The grass was turning indigo beneath the violet twilight; the white marble dome of the Victoria Memorial rose in the distance like an anchored cloud; the sodium streetlights along Red Road were flickering on in long, parallel avenues of amber fire.\n\nBhabesh rested his elbow against the open window frame.\n\nFor thirty-one years, this forty-five-minute journey across the green heart of the city had been his decompression chamber.\n\nIn this carriage, between BBD Bagh and the bridge at Park Circus, the weight of the export manifests had fallen from his shoulders. He would watch the children playing cricket in the dusk, the horses from the Calcutta Police mounted division being led back to their stables behind the racecourse, the office workers sitting on plastic sheets sharing cones of roasted chickpeas.\n\nBy the time the tram reached Gariahat, he had ceased to be the Chief Documentation Officer. He had become simply Bhabesh—a man who bought six sweet lemons from the fruit stall outside the tram depot, carried them upstairs to the second-floor flat in Garia, took off his shoes, and asked his wife whether his daughter had called from her engineering college in Kalyani.\n\nNow, his daughter was thirty-two.\n\nShe was married to a software architect in Frankfurt; she had a four-year-old son named Leo who spoke German and English and only five words of Bengali; she lived in an apartment with radiant underfloor heating and triple-glazed windows that kept out every sound of the European winter.\n\nWhen Bhabesh had spoken to her on video last Sunday, she had said: \"Baba, now that you are retiring, you and Ma must come to Germany in May. You can stay for three months. We will take you to see the Rhine castles.\"\n\nBhabesh had looked at his grandson on the screen, holding a plastic toy fire engine, and felt a strange, chilling panic.\n\nWhat would he do in Germany for three months?\n\nIn Germany, the cargo cleared the port of Hamburg in six seconds through automated blockchain ledgers. In Germany, there was no Ramu boiling tea over an open stove; there was no Haripada pulling a brass elevator lever; there was no Baithakkhana Road smelling of bone glue and calfskin.\n\nHe would be an exhibit in his daughter's life—a polite, silent old man sitting on an Ikea sofa, looking out at the manicured lawns of a Frankfurt suburb where the trains arrived with silent, terrifying precision and nobody ever needed forty minutes to decide whether the tea was safe.",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1486406146926-c627a92ad1ab?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=85",
      "alt": "An old green and cream tram moving through tree-lined avenue at dusk",
      "caption": "Tram Route 25 crossing the Maidan toward Gariahat at 18:35.",
