      "chapterTitle": "VII-C. The Flat in Garia and the Evening Light",
      "heading": "VII-C. The Flat in Garia and the Evening Light",
      "body": "It was seven-fifteen when Bhabesh reached the three-storey brick apartment building on Raja S. C. Mullick Road in Garia.\n\nHe climbed the concrete stairs to the second floor, his leather Bata shoes making a slow, rhythmic clatter on the terrazzo risers. On the second-floor landing, the smell of mustard oil, fried cumin, and parwal curry was drifting from under the wooden door of Flat 2-A.\n\nHe did not ring the bell.\n\nHe took his brass flat key from his trouser pocket, inserted it into the Godrej rim lock, and turned it twice to the right.\n\nThe door opened with a quiet click.\n\nThe living room was illuminated by the warm, forty-watt yellow bulb in the corner standing lamp. His wife, Anindita, was sitting on the cane sofa with a steel basin on her lap, snapping the ends off fresh string beans for dinner.\n\nShe looked up.\n\nShe did not ask him how the farewell was. She did not ask about the speech or the brass commemorative plate. She looked at his face, at his shoulders, and at the cardboard box he was carrying in both arms.\n\n\"You have brought the box,\" she said softly, setting the basin of beans on the glass coffee table.\n\n\"I have brought the box,\" Bhabesh said.\n\nHe set the box down on the dining table.\n\nHe lifted out the contents one by one: the backup spectacles in the velvet tin; the green woollen muffler knitted by his mother in 1994; the old identity card with the yellowed cellotape; the red commemorative shawl in its cellophane wrapper; the yellow gladioli bouquet whose stems were now dry; and the brass ship's anchor.\n\nAnindita picked up the brass anchor.\n\nShe felt its weight—two pounds and four ounces of solid admiralty metal. She held it with both hands, the way she had held their daughter thirty-two years earlier on the verandah of the maternity clinic in Elgin Road.\n\n\"Where will you put it?\" she asked.\n\nBhabesh looked around the living room.\n\nAlong the south wall stood the wooden showcase that had been part of her dowry in 1991—a glass-fronted cabinet containing their wedding silver, two porcelain cups from their honeymoon in Darjeeling, a clay idol of Ganesha from Kumartuli, and three school prize trophies their daughter had won for English recitation in the eighth standard.\n\nBhabesh pointed to the bottom shelf, between the Ganesha and the Darjeeling teacups.\n\n\"There,\" Bhabesh said. \"Beside the Ganesha. It can hold down the lace doily so the fan doesn't blow it away.\"\n\nAnindita placed the anchor on the shelf. She adjusted it until the engraved inscription—*Steadfast in Record, 1993–2008*—faced forward into the room.\n\nShe closed the glass door of the cabinet. The latch clicked shut.\n\n\"Go wash your hands,\" Anindita said, her voice returning to its normal, domestic cadence. \"The rice is hot. I made the fish curry with green chilies the way you like.\"\n\nBhabesh walked into the bathroom.\n\nHe turned on the brass tap. He washed his hands with Lifebuoy soap, scrubbing the dark ink callus on the middle finger of his right hand until the skin was red.\n\nHe looked at his face in the mirror above the washbasin.\n\nThe man in the mirror had silver hair at the temples, deep parentheses around his mouth, and two clear, dark eyes that looked back at him without fear, without regret, and without the protective armor of an official title.\n\nTomorrow morning, there would be no 07:14 suburban local. There would be no 09:15 rotation of the brass revolving door. There would be no Haripada pulling the iron cage shut.\n\nThere would only be the morning newspaper, a cup of tea made with two spoons of sugar, and forty years of sunlight waiting on the balcony.\n\nHe dried his hands on the cotton towel, walked into the kitchen, and sat down at the table.",
